<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gustav Klimt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Klimt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gustav Klimt was an Austrian painter and a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-secession">Vienna Secession</a>, known for decorative works such as The Kiss. He was one of the central artists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>'s cultural flowering around 1900. The site presents him as part of the artistic and intellectual life of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> in its final decades.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustav-klimt.jpg" alt="Gustav Klimt" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josef Anton Trčka · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Klimt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gustav Klimt was born on 14 July 1862 in Baumgarten, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the second of seven children. His father, Ernst Klimt the Elder, was a gold engraver from a peasant family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> who often struggled to find work, and the family lived in poverty and moved address at least five times between 1862 and 1884. Klimt's brother Ernst, later an engraver like their father, enrolled with him at the Kunstgewerbeschule. With their friend Franz von Matsch, Klimt and Ernst formed by 1880 a team called the Künstlercompagnie, which secured many commissions, including murals in the Kunsthistorisches Museum and decorations for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> built by the firm Fellner &amp; Helmer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1897 Klimt became a founding member and president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-secession">Vienna Secession</a> and of its periodical, Ver Sacrum. The group aimed to give exhibitions to unconventional young artists and to bring the best foreign artists to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, and it declared no manifesto, so that Naturalists, Realists and Symbolists coexisted. Klimt painted his version of its symbol, Pallas Athena, in 1898, and he remained with the Secession until 1908. His paintings for the ceiling of the Great Hall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a>, completed around 1900, were criticised as pornographic, after which he accepted no more public commissions.</p><p>From 1900 Klimt became known above all as a painter of women, producing about one large portrait a year in which flatness, decoration and gold leaf followed the principles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a>. His golden phase included Judith I (1901), Portrait of Adele Bloch-Bauer I (1907), The Kiss (1907–08) and the Stoclet Frieze (1905–1911), and it brought positive critical reaction and financial success. In 1911 his Death and Life won first prize at the world exhibition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. On 11 January 1918 he suffered a stroke that paralysed his right side, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 6 February from pneumonia brought on by an early case of Spanish flu, aged 55, leaving many paintings unfinished.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Klimt's primary subject was the female body, and his work is marked by frank eroticism. He treated Old Testament heroines as dangerous femmes fatales and took life, love and death as important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a>. Among the Secession artists he was the most influenced by Japanese art. He also painted landscapes. His work was an important influence on the younger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egon-schiele">Egon Schiele</a>, and Klimt supported Schiele's 1917 plan for an artists' association, the Kunsthalle, which failed for lack of funding.</p><p>The Nazis looted many of Klimt's works during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and several were destroyed. In 2006 an Austrian arbitration board ordered five paintings, among them Portrait of Adele Bloch-Bauer I, returned to an heir of the Jewish family that had owned them, and that portrait was then sold to Ronald Lauder for the Neue Galerie New York.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gustav Klimt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vienna's fin-de-siècle culture rested on this prosperity: the Ringstrasse, the Secession movement, coffee-house intellectual life and the professional class that produced Sigmund Freud, Gustav Mahler, <b>Gustav Klimt</b> and Theodor Herzl all belonged to a prosperous, cosmopolitan urban economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Baumgarten, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.2,16.27&amp;year=1898" aria-label="Baumgarten on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4928 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gustav Klimt"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5378 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5378 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.2,16.27&amp;year=1898">What was here in 1898? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
